				\begin{document}$$\mathit{ln}\left[-\mathit{ln}\left(1-X\right)\right]= n\;lnt+\mathit{ln}\;k$$\end{document} where  X  is the reacted fraction (0 <  X  < 1) of the desorbed hydrogen,  t  is the reaction time,  n  is the Avrami exponent, and  k  is the rate constant. Figure  9  shows the JMAK plots (ln[-ln(1-X)] vs. ln t) for all samples. The experimental data fitted well with the linear regression lines (R 2  > 0.98), indicating that the desorption mechanism is consistent with the nucleation and growth model. The reaction fraction curves are presented in Fig.  9 a. A distinct trend is observed where the curves for the Ni-containing composites shift significantly to the left compared to pure AZ31 and the Ti-containing samples. This shift to the left means that it takes less time to reach the same desorption fraction, which quantitatively proves that nickel is a better catalyst for speeding up reaction kinetics. The corresponding JMAK linear plots are shown in Fig.  9 b, which exhibit a linear relationship fitting the equation form  y  =  ax  +  b . In this context, the slope (a) corresponds to the Avrami exponent ( n ), and the intercept (b) represents the natural logarithm of the rate constant (ln k). The calculated  n  values for the composites fall within the range of 1.5 to 2.0. According to the JMAK theory, this range corresponds to diffusion-controlled growth with a zero nucleation rate (instantaneous nucleation). This kinetic behavior is inherently associated with the microstructural alterations caused by high-energy ball milling. When you mill something, the severe plastic deformation creates a lot of crystal defects, grain boundaries, and dislocations. These structural flaws are great places for the hydride phase to start growing. Figure 9 JMAK analysis of hydrogen desorption kinetics:  a – c  reacted fraction (X) versus time and  d – f  corresponding JMAK plots at 375, 350, and 325 °C.

